# GhostFile Project Rules (Next.js 15/16 + Binary Processing)

## Tech Stack Preferences
- Framework: Next.js 15+ (App Router)
- Language: TypeScript (Strict mode)
- Styling: Tailwind CSS 4+
- Components: Lucide React for icons, Radix UI primitives
- State Management: Zustand (only if needed, prefer React State/Context for local file flow)
- Logic: High-performance binary processing (ArrayBuffer, TypedArrays, Web Workers)

## Coding Standards
- Use Server Components (RSC) by default. Use 'use client' only for interactivity (Dropzone, Canvas, Maps).
- Use Server Actions for file metadata extraction that requires Node.js-only libraries.
- For binary manipulation, prefer native JS (Uint8Array, DataView) over heavy libraries.
- No 'any' type. Define strict interfaces for all file metadata and binary structures.

## File Handling & Performance
- Never block the main thread. If a file is > 10MB, suggest using a Web Worker.
- Use ReadableStream for processing large files to keep memory usage low.
- Always implement clean-up: Revoke object URLs (URL.revokeObjectURL) to prevent memory leaks.

## UI/UX Guidelines (Industrial Tech Aesthetic)
- Follow the "Engineering Brutalism" style: sharp borders, monospace fonts for data, high contrast.
- Use Lucide icons with strokeWidth={1.5}.
- Every action must have a clear loading state (Streaming SSR or skeleton).
- Responsive design: Desktop-first (since it's an engineering tool), but mobile-friendly.

## Specific Constraints for GhostFile
- Detect file type by "Magic Numbers" (File Signatures), not just file extensions.
- When extracting GPS, always prepare data in [lat, lng] format for Mapbox/Leaflet.
- The "Purge" function must recreate the binary without metadata segments (e.g., stripping APP1 markers in JPEG).

## Communication Style
- Be concise. Don't explain basic React hooks unless asked.
- If a suggested library is heavy, provide a lightweight native alternative.
- Prioritize security: warn about potential XSS or malicious file structures.

- Comments in code only in English